  "description": {
   "content": [
    {
     "content": [
      {
       "text": "ActiveMQ has been designed with the possibility to deploy an",
       "type": "text"
      },
      {
       "marks": [
        {
         "attrs": {
          "href": "https://activemq.apache.org/web-console"
         },
         "type": "link"
        }
       ],
       "text": " integrated webconsole",
       "type": "text"
      },
      {
       "text": " to monitor queues and topics through a web UI, and send special management events to those, such as purge messages through a simple click. This is a quite convenient tool to check the progress of indexation and overall to manage ActiveMQ. As the ActiveMQ instance of Fedora does not come bundled with a webconsole, it currently requires a separate instance of ActiveMQ and its webconsole to be “connected” through a RMI server port to monitor Fedora’s queues and topics. ",
       "type": "text"
      }
     ],
     "type": "paragraph"
    },
    {
     "content": [
      {
       "type": "hardBreak"
      },
      {
       "text": "I think it would make sense to allow the deployment of such a webconsole directly through Fedora, maybe on the user side it could be enabled through a configuration property. It is quite a handy tool, and it is a bit conter-productive to deploy a separate ActiveMQ instance just to get that UI working with Fedora.",
       "type": "text"
      }
     ],
     "type": "paragraph"
    }
   ],
